{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2502.00012, what challenge do policymakers face when deciding how to act on information about high-risk AI systems under the EU AI Act, and what solution is proposed?", "answer": "Policymakers struggle to interpret and act on information about high-risk AI systems; the document suggests relaxing informational thresholds and using 'satisficing' for earlier intervention.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "deciding how to interpret the information gathered, and whether (or how) to act upon it, still presents a significant challenge for policymakers."}, {"page": 9, "quote": "to intervene effectively policymakers may need to relax the policy-relevant informational threshold and resort to 'satisficing' (172)-i.e., making governance decisions on the basis of incomplete information collected at an earlier stage in the technology's development and use (132)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2502.00012, how does the precautionary principle relate to AI governance, and what complexity-related consideration modifies its application?", "answer": "The precautionary principle supports preemptive AI regulation; complexity theory suggests it should be applied when risks may spread swiftly and exponentially, increasing the cost of delay.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "The principle, which is used in environmental governance and public health policy, supports preemptive regulatory intervention before harms have (fully) materialized or risks are established conclusively, often requiring actors interested in pursuing a potentially risky activity to first prove its safety (173, 174)."}, {"page": 10, "quote": "insights from complexity can help refine and calibrate the precautionary principle. In particular, regulators should consider whether the relevant risks will likely spread swiftly and exponentially and thereby pose grave systemic risk (132)."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2502.00012, what does the abbreviation 'NIST' stand for in the context of AI governance frameworks?", "answer": "NIST stands for the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology AI Risk Management Framework (January 2023) (145)"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2502.00012, what is the adaptation mechanism for the U.S. Executive Order on AI, and how can it be changed?", "answer": "The U.S. Executive Order on AI's requirements are carried out by agencies with discretion and can be modified or revoked by the President.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Requirements carried out by various government agencies that exercise sig- nificant discretion. Like other US exec- utive orders, the order can be modified or revoked by the President-and was revoked by President Trump in Jan- uary 2025 (149)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2502.00012, what is the main criticism of the precautionary principle when applied to AI regulation?", "answer": "The main criticism is that it does not withstand cost-benefit analysis and may unduly limit or forgo gains from new technology.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "A prominent criticism of the precautionary principle-which in the case of AI may require robust technical safety guarantees (175, 176) or other forms of assurance (177, 178) -is that it does not withstand cost-benefit analysis, i.e., it unduly limits or forgoes the gains from new technology (170, 179, 180)."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2502.00012, what term describes making regulatory decisions with incomplete information at an early stage of AI development?", "answer": "The term is 'satisficing,' meaning decisions are made based on incomplete information collected early in the technology's development.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "to intervene effectively policymakers may need to relax the policy-relevant informational threshold and resort to 'satisficing' (172)-i.e., making governance decisions on the basis of incomplete information collected at an earlier stage in the technology's development and use (132)."}]}
]}
